  <artifactId>s07-third-party-integration</artifactId>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>s07-third-party-integration</name>
  <description>S7: integrating with a system nobody here controls, in both directions.</description>

  <!--
    Two modules, and only one of them is ours.

    S4 and S6 pair two services we own, so the interesting question there is what the contract between
    them should be. Here the second module is a stand-in for a payment gateway: a system with its own
    vocabulary, its own reliability, and no interest in our conventions. It is deliberately built the
    way a foreign system is encountered rather than the way we would build one — plain Java, no Spring,
    no DDD, ISO-8583-flavoured response codes — because an anticorruption layer that faces something
    designed by the same hands is not being tested.

    It is a real module rather than a test fixture for two reasons. It runs standalone (`java -jar`), so
    the whole flow including the callback can be driven by hand; and the callback signing contract lives
    in exactly one place, on the sending side, which is where the client half of a webhook contract
    belongs. The payment service depends on it in `test` scope only.
  -->
  <modules>
    <module>gateway-stub</module>
    <module>payment-service</module>
  </modules>
</project>
